meta {
  name: Update Application Settings
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

put {
  url: {{base_url}}/settings
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "dark_theme": {
      "color_bg_base": "#141414",
      "color_bg_container": "#1f1f1f",
      "color_border": "#424242",
      "color_link": "#1668dc",
      "color_primary": "#61dafb",
      "color_text_base": "#ffffff",
      "color_text_description": "#bfbfbf"
    },
    "language_code": "fa",
    "light_theme": {
      "color_bg_base": "#ffffff",
      "color_bg_container": "#ffffff",
      "color_border": "#d9d9d9",
      "color_link": "#1677ff",
      "color_primary": "#61dafb",
      "color_text_base": "#000000",
      "color_text_description": "#8c8c8c"
    }
  }
}

docs {
  ## Purpose

  Updates global branding and optionally the system-wide language.

  ## Authentication

  Requires an authenticated system administrator or an account with `settings.update`.
  Only a system administrator may change the persisted language.

  ## Behavior

  - `language_code` is optional. A system administrator may send a code from `GET /languages` or omit it to retain the existing value.
  - Other settings editors should omit `language_code`; an explicitly unchanged value is accepted for compatibility but is never written by their save. A changed value is forbidden.
  - Logo identifiers are optional public file identifiers returned by `POST /files`.
  - The PUT replaces both palettes and logo selections. Send existing `dark_logo.id`/`light_logo.id` as `dark_logo_id`/`light_logo_id` when retaining logos during an unrelated save; omitting either identifier clears that selection.
  - Both complete palettes are required and the operation is idempotent.

  ## Response

  - `200 OK` — Settings updated successfully.
  - `400 Bad Request` — Submitted settings are invalid.
    The saved `400.json` example is a missing `dark_theme` with the persisted language set to English; errors contain a localized `message`, without a field-error `cause` object. Omitted `language_code` alone is valid.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — Authentication is missing or invalid.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `settings.update` is missing or a non-system-administrator attempts to change language.
  - `404 Not Found` — A language or logo does not exist.
}
